TI  - Acalculous Cholecystitis: An Unusual Manifestation of Cytomegalovirus Disease in Renal Transplant Recipient
AU  - M. Habib Allah
AU  - Nour Houda Bassit
AU  - W. Fadili
AU  - I. Laouad
JO  - Open Journal of Nephrology
PY  - 2013
VL  - 03
IS  - 02
SP  - 115
EP  - 116
DO  - 10.4236/ojneph.2013.32020
AB  - Background: Common clinical manifestations of cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection \ninclude flu-like symptoms with fever, diarrhea, leukopenia, and elevated liver \nenzymes. Diagnosis is made by detection of the virus by buffy-coat blood culture \nor by polymerase chain reaction (PCR) analysis. Methods: Here we describe a women \nrenal transplant recipient who presented with acalculous cholecystitis with CMV \nviremia, anemia and leucopenia tree months after she received a kidney from \ncadaveric donor. Results: Retrospective analysis of peripheral blood by \nPCR analysis was positive for CMV DNA. Treatement with Intravenous Ganciclovir \nwas started after diagnosis. The role of cholecystectomy in patients diagnosed \nas having acalculous cholecystitis associated with systemic CMV disease remains \nunclear. Conclusions: Because CMV infection is common in transplant \npatients, the atypical manifestations of CMV should be considered in the differential \ndiagnosis of posttransplant complications. Detection of CMV DNA in the \nperipheral blood by PCR analysis may help identify these patients.
ER  - 

